(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ent-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Advanced ENT History & Examination Quiz 2 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient's own voice is UNUSUALLY LOUD. Which kind of hearing loss does that suggest, and why?","choices":["Neither — loud voice indicates a psychiatric cause","Sensorineural","Conductive — the middle ear amplifies the patient's own voice","Either — voice volume does not distinguish them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Voice volume is a feedback loop: the worse you hear yourself, the louder you speak. The patient cannot hear themselves well, so they raise their voice. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What does GENERALISED lymphadenopathy raise?","choices":["Otitis media and otitis externa","Thyroiditis and goitre","Human immunodeficiency virus","Dental infection and tonsillitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Generalised disease means a systemic process rather than drainage from one infected site. Epstein-Barr virus, lymphoma, leukaemia and sarcoidosis. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Why should dentures be removed during the oral examination?","choices":["To assess the fit of the denture itself","To allow the tongue blade to be inserted","To let the patient speak more clearly during the history","To look underneath for ulcers and lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A denture hides exactly the mucosa most at risk from chronic irritation. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which causes produce CENTRAL vertigo?","choices":["Otitis media, otitis externa and Eustachian tube dysfunction","Brainstem lesion","Benign positional vertigo, Ménière disease and labyrinthitis","Loop diuretics, aminoglycosides, salicylates and alcohol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all vascular, demyelinating or structural problems above the inner ear. Atherosclerosis, multiple sclerosis, vertebrobasilar migraine and transient ischaemic attack. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A patient says others mumble, and that they struggle most in a noisy restaurant. Which kind of hearing loss does that pattern suggest?","choices":["Sensorineural","Conductive — noise masks the residual hearing","Mixed loss, which always presents this way","Neither — this is a description of tinnitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pattern is loss of discrimination rather than loss of volume, and background noise competes with what little discrimination remains. Trouble understanding speech, and worse in noise. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which features mark vertigo as CENTRAL rather than peripheral?","choices":["Hearing impaired on one side with tinnitus present","Episodes lasting under a minute triggered by head position","Aural fullness with fluctuating tinnitus","Hearing unaffected, tinnitus absent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The absence of ear symptoms alongside the presence of neurological ones is the pattern. Other brainstem deficits such as dysarthria, ataxia or crossed motor and sensory deficits. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why does a septal haematoma require URGENT drainage?","choices":["To prevent secondary sinusitis","To prevent necrosis of the septal cartilage","To prevent the blood tracking into the orbit","To prevent airway obstruction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cartilage has no blood supply of its own — it depends on the lining that the haematoma has stripped away, so the clock starts immediately. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which cranial nerves carry referred pain to the ear?","choices":["III, IV and VI","VIII alone","XI and XII","V, VII, IX and X"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Four nerves supply sensation to the ear, which is exactly why disease well away from the ear can present as otalgia. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the canal look like in ACUTE otitis externa?","choices":["Swollen, narrow, moist, pale and tender, and it may be erythematous","Thickened, red and itchy","Dry, scaly and pale, with no tenderness","Normal, with the abnormality confined to the tympanic membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The swelling and tenderness dominate, and the narrowing is often what makes the examination difficult. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How do you confirm the otoscope has no leaks BEFORE inserting it for insufflation?","choices":["Compare the bulb pressure against the other otoscope","Place a finger over the speculum tip and squeeze the bulb","Squeeze the bulb in open air and listen for a hiss","Insert the speculum and watch the membrane; no movement means a leak"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If the bulb squeezes freely with the tip blocked, air is escaping somewhere and the test will be uninterpretable. Resistance means no leak. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How does a septal haematoma form?","choices":["Chronic inflammation erodes the septum until it bleeds","Venous engorgement of the turbinates spreads to the septum","Injury disrupts the vessels","Blood collects within the cartilage itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The separation is the problem, not the volume of blood. Pulling the lining away from the cartilage so blood collects between the two. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which three structures are inspected and palpated before the otoscope is picked up?","choices":["The parotid gland, the temporomandibular joint and the zygomatic arch","The preauricular, postauricular and occipital nodes","The auricles, the mastoid and the tragus","The tympanic membrane, the ossicles and the cochlea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tenderness found here localises disease before anything is put into the canal, and tragal tenderness in particular points to the canal itself. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What result on the whispered voice test counts as normal, and what counts as abnormal?","choices":["Normal is six of six correct; anything less is abnormal","Normal is one of six correct","Normal is any response at all; the test is qualitative","Normal is three or more of six correct"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The thresholds are stated as a fraction of six because the three-item sequence is given twice. Abnormal is four of six incorrect. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What usually causes a tympanic membrane perforation, and what may accompany it?","choices":["Cerumen impaction, with pain through the perforation","Otitis media, and drainage may come through the perforation","Otitis externa, with itching through the perforation","Barotrauma, with vertigo through the perforation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The perforation is usually the consequence of middle ear disease rather than a primary event. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How long does benign positional vertigo last overall, as opposed to how long a single episode lasts?","choices":["Episodes last hours, and the condition lasts a few weeks","Episodes and the condition both last a few weeks","Each episode is under a minute, but the condition lasts a few weeks and may recur","Each episode is under a minute, and the condition resolves within 24 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The table separates the two timescales, and confusing them is the usual error. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A raised, shiny, slow-growing lesion on the auricle with visible telangiectasia. What should be suspected?","choices":["A tophus","Basal cell carcinoma","A cutaneous cyst","A keloid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Raised, shiny, telangiectatic and slow growing — and malignant, which is what separates it from the benign lumps in the same list. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"The Weber test is done and the patient hears the tone only in the LEFT ear. Which ear has sensorineural hearing loss?","choices":["Neither — this is a normal result","Both","The right","The left"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sensorineural loss lateralises AWAY from the damaged side, so hearing the tone on the left places the lesion on the right. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A patient speaks unusually SOFTLY despite a hearing complaint. Which loss does that point to?","choices":["Presbycusis specifically","Conductive — the inner ear and cochlear nerve are intact","Sensorineural — the patient compensates by speaking quietly","Mixed loss only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and bone conduction of their own voice is if anything enhanced, so they have no reason to speak up. So the patient hears their own voice normally. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which headache patterns are EPISODIC, and which are UNILATERAL?","choices":["Migraine and cluster are episodic; migraine and tension are unilateral","All three are episodic and all three unilateral","Only cluster is episodic and only tension unilateral","Migraine and tension are episodic; migraine and cluster are unilateral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Migraine appears in both lists, which is why the two features have to be taken together rather than either alone. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What kind of pressure is applied to the bulb during pneumatic otoscopy?","choices":["As hard as the patient will tolerate","A series of rapid repeated squeezes","Quick, firm but gentle","Slow and sustained, held for several seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Only a small pressure change is needed; the membrane's movement is slight, and force adds nothing but discomfort. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Where do tongue cancers usually appear, and in whom?","choices":["On the lateral border or undersurface","On the dorsum, as smooth red patches, in females under thirty","At the tip, as painful ulcers, in any age group","On the undersurface, as blue tortuous swellings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Those two sites are exactly the ones a casual look misses, which is why the tongue is lifted and held. As indurated red or white lesions, in males over fifty. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which causes of hoarseness should a history cover?","choices":["Viral laryngitis","Allergic rhinitis, sinusitis and postnasal drip","Gingivitis and dental caries","Presbycusis and tinnitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a list that runs from the trivial to the serious, which is why hoarseness always earns a full history. Voice overuse, laryngeal nerve damage, reflux and smoking. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Why does the ABSENCE of cough count toward the Centor score rather than its presence?","choices":["A cough indicates the infection has spread to the lower airway","Cough is a criterion only in children","A cough points toward a viral cause","Coughing masks tonsillar exudates on examination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The rule is built to separate bacterial from viral pharyngitis, and cough is the most useful viral marker in the set. So its absence raises the probability of streptococcal infection. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Necrotising ulcerative gingivitis is known by which other names?","choices":["Ludwig's angina, and quinsy","Angular cheilitis, and perleche","Aphthous stomatitis, and canker sores","Vincent's angina, and trench mouth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The second name records where it was first described in numbers, in the trenches of the First World War. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What causes a PERFORATED nasal septum?","choices":["Trauma, surgery or drug use","Allergic rhinitis and chronic sinusitis","A foreign body lodged for several days","Repeated epistaxis from Kiesselbach's plexus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three destroy the septal blood supply or the cartilage itself, and drug use is the one patients do not volunteer. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient says they are “dizzy”. Which four categories must the history separate?","choices":["Vertigo, migraine, seizure and syncope","Peripheral vertigo, central vertigo, tinnitus and hearing loss","Orthostatic, cardiac, neurological and metabolic","Vertigo, presyncope, disequilibrium and psychiatric causes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The word means nothing until the patient explains it, and these are the four things it turns out to mean. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A tympanic membrane perforation that does NOT extend to the margin is called what?","choices":["Central","Marginal","Attic","Traumatic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classification is simply by whether the margin is involved, and a marginal perforation is the one that does involve it. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A diffusely enlarged, FIRM thyroid points to what?","choices":["Hashimoto thyroiditis","Graves disease","A multinodular goitre","Thyroid carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Firmness is the feature that separates it from Graves, where the gland is soft. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What frequency pattern does presbycusis take?","choices":["Higher frequency loss","Lower frequency loss","All frequencies equally","Loss confined to the speech range, sparing higher and lower tones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is why the complaint is about clarity rather than volume — the high registers carry the consonants. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the Rinne result in CONDUCTIVE hearing loss, and why?","choices":["Bone conduction is heard but air conduction is heard longer","Bone conduction equal to or greater than air conduction","Air conduction greater than bone conduction","Neither is heard at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The bone route goes round the obstruction and reaches an intact cochlea directly. Because vibration through bone bypasses the blocked external or middle ear. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 44"}]};
